<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/87-bce">87 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo was a Roman general and politician, the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> the Great. As consul in 89 BCE he commanded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>, captured the rebel city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> after a long siege, and passed a law granting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rights to Transpadane <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. He died in 87 BCE during the civil war between Marius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo was born around 135 BCE into a noble family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The cognomen Strabo means cross-eyed. His mother, Lucilia, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suessa-aurunca">Suessa Aurunca</a> and was a sister of the satirical poet Gaius Lucilius, and his father was Sextus Pompeius. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> had become the richest and most prominent family of the region, with a large clientele and influence in Picenum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which the Senate could not ignore despite its prejudice against rural families. After military service, probably as a tribune, Strabo rose through the offices and governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> as promagistrate in 93 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> he commanded against the Italian rebels in the north. In 90 BCE a large force of Picentes, Vestini and Marsi attacked his marching legions, and he was blockaded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a>. In the autumn he launched two sorties that caught his enemies in a pincer, and the survivors fell back on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a>, which he set out to starve. The success made him popular and he was elected consul for 89 BCE with Lucius Porcius Cato. He defeated a rebel column marching into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, killing 5,000 men, and another 5,000 died trying to recross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>.</p><p>Cato was killed attacking the enemy camp near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fucine-lake">Fucine Lake</a>, which left Strabo sole consul. He defeated an Italian relief army of 60,000 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a>. After the city fell he had the rebel leaders whipped and executed and auctioned off their goods, keeping the proceeds, which may explain his reputation for greed.</p><p>He triumphed on 27 December 89 BCE. When his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> ended he kept his army in the field, and the Senate transferred it to the consul Quintus Pompeius Rufus, whom Strabo's soldiers murdered. He stayed out of events when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 88 BCE. In 87 BCE he answered the Senate's request for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> and Lucius Cornelius Cinna, but he played the sides against each other, and Publius Rutilius Rufus called him the vilest man alive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 87 BCE Strabo camped outside the Colline Gate, where disease broke out among his troops. He caught dysentery and died within days. His avarice and cruelty had made his soldiers hate him so much that they dragged his corpse from the bier and through the streets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> told another story, that he was struck by lightning. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> the Great took the legions back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a> and used them to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> a few years later. Strabo also had a daughter, Pompeia, and the cities of Alba Pompeia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-vecchio">Laus Pompeia</a> were named in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sulla, <b>Pompeius Strabo</b> and Marius defeated the remaining forces, and Silo was killed in 88 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/italia-social-war-confederation">Italia (Social War confederation)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Regio V Picenum</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-97" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-97">What was here in 97 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
